# Tolgee at the fixed revision 6ddba66 (CVE-2024-52297, tolgee/tolgee-platform,
# kotlin). The public configuration endpoint is
# ConfigurationController.getPublicConfiguration, a @RestController mapped at
# /api/public/ with @CrossOrigin(origins = ["*"]) and no authentication, whose
# @GetMapping("configuration") handler returns a PublicConfigurationDTO. That
# DTO's primary constructor declared the application's whole configuration
# object as a serialized member:
#
#     class PublicConfigurationDTO(
#       @Schema(hidden = true)
#       val properties: TolgeeProperties,
#       ...
#
# TolgeeProperties is the @ConfigurationProperties root for the deployment and
# carries smtp.password, the S3/Azure content-storage credentials,
# machineTranslation.{google,aws,deepl,azure,baidu} API keys, slack.token and
# slack.signingSecret, recaptcha.secretKey, sendInBlue/mailjet keys and
# postHog.apiKey. In Kotlin a `val` constructor parameter is a property, so
# Jackson serialises it into the response body; @Schema(hidden = true) hides
# it from the OpenAPI document only, not from the payload. The fixed revision drops the
# `val`, leaving `properties` a plain constructor parameter the body still
# reads to derive the deliberately-public scalars (appName, maxUploadFileSize,
# recaptcha.siteKey, ...) that belong there, and deletes the
# now-unused private isContentDeliveryEnabled helper. The remediation is
# present at the fixed revision and the construct is present at its parent.
#
# Nothing in the dataflow changes across the fixed revision -- the controller passes
# `properties = configuration` to the same constructor on both revisions, and
# only whether that member is serialised differs -- so the discriminator is a
# declaration property of the constructor parameter and nothing else.
#
# Measured, bounded subtree `backend/api/src/main/kotlin` (256 files, the
# module carrying the change), three `--cache off` runs per revision at both
# the original and the current definitions state: 4 findings on the parent and
# 4 on the fixed revision, byte-stable inside each revision and identical
# across the revision boundary. The four rows are VYQL-AUTH-079 on
# ProjectsController.kt (permissionService.revoke), VYQL-AUTH-002 on
# V2InvitationController.kt, VYQL-INJ-004 from StartBatchJobController into
# MtResultStreamer, VYQL-AUTH-001 on V2UserController.kt -- none on
# PublicConfigurationDTO.kt or ConfigurationController.kt. The review-flag
# view holds 6 flags (3 auth, 3 resource-release) on each revision, likewise
# naming neither file, and a whole-`backend` scan of the parent reports 31
# findings with no row naming either file. `vyql match` on the vulnerable file
# and on the controller labels no source, sink or control at all.
#
# The enumeration: 48 classes in the tree's main sources declare a
# `val`/`var` constructor parameter typed as TolgeeProperties, the
# @ConfigurationProperties root, alone. One is this DTO. The other 47 are
# constructor-injected services, assemblers, filters, cache and Slack
# components -- the ordinary Spring idiom for consuming configuration -- and
# widening the type to the other @ConfigurationProperties classes pushes the
# count past 60. So even once the declaration fact below exists, a binding
# needs the response-DTO context and not the parameter shape alone, or it
# fires dozens of times for one true positive.
#
# The blocker is a Kotlin frontend gap. `class_declaration` is lowered by
# taking only the class body (kotlin.go:77, `c.decls(c.classBody(n))`), and
# the `primary_constructor` -> `class_parameters` -> `class_parameter` subtree
# is never visited: the `val`/`var` keyword that makes a parameter a
# serialised property, the parameter's `user_type`, and its
# `modifiers`/`annotation` (where @Schema(hidden = true) lives) all reach
# nothing. The grammar puts the keyword there as an unnamed `val`/`var` child
# of `class_parameter` (tree-sitter-kotlin v1.1.0 node-types.json), so a walk
# has to read it off the raw token. c.params and c.paramTypes (kotlin.go:373,
# :392) match only a direct `function_value_parameters` child, which a class
# declaration does not have, and ktParamEntries (kotlin.go:496) is called only
# for function declarations -- which is why a @GetMapping handler's parameter
# does carry annotation tokens (verified: the shipped kotlin.input binding
# fires on a handler parameter) while a constructor parameter carries none.
# Verified directly at the current definitions state: `vyql graph` on a pair
# of files differing only in that keyword produces byte-identical node and
# edge sets, no node exists at any primary-constructor parameter line in
# either revision, and the only facts a DTO class emits are its
# analysis.class.context node plus the class body's property initialisers.
# Probe bindings on `node.context.classAnnotation contains "Schema"` and
# `node.context.annotation contains "Schema"` label zero nodes while a
# positive control on the same class-context nodes fires eight.
#
# Not mechanical. Lowering the primary constructor adds a new fact class
# (constructor-parameter property-ness, declared type, parameter annotations)
# and, for a `val` parameter, a new dataflow relation from the constructor
# argument into the class-body property reads -- today those reads resolve
# against a free-floating Name with no definition. It changes the frontend for
# every idiomatic Kotlin class, not one function or a small region, so it is
# a design change rather than a localized correction of a construct the engine
# handles. Nothing half-built is waiting to be corrected either, the way the
# dead `prefix_expression`/`postfix_expression` cases were for unary
# expressions: the class_declaration arm has no constructor path at all, so
# the change invents where the facts live (nir.ClassDef carries no param
# entries, and parameter-entry events are function-frame-bound in lowering).
# It is also not sufficient on its own: a consuming binding still needs the
# response-DTO context to escape the enumeration above, which in Kotlin needs
# either the per-language review classifier or function/class-member context
# tokens -- both separately recorded frontend gaps -- so this construct and
# those together are a sweep, not one rank's fix.
#
# Reuse is checked before concluding, and re-checked at the current
# definitions state. code.ConfigExposureReview
# (VYQL-SEC-041, CWE-200/312/522/532, "configuration or diagnostic
# serialization may expose inline secrets without an observed redaction path")
# is exactly the judgement class for this shape and is the rule these cases
# pin; its only bindings are Python (a pythonReview classifier keyed on
# `config_exposure_without_api_redaction`) and Go (MarshalConfig callee paths
# and a type-context field pair) -- the Kotlin surface carries nothing for
# configuration or response serialization, and the Python shape shows a
# Kotlin binding of this class waits on the classifier gap as well as the
# constructor fact.
#
# code.SensitiveDataInResponse keys on credential material reaching a
# response, which is the field-level form; here the whole configuration
# object is the member and no field is named. code.DataExposureSmell is a
# route returning an unpicked database record. code.InternalContentExposureReview
# is persisted internal documents. None fits better, and all of them are
# blocked by the same missing fact.
#
# All cases assert reject VYQL-SEC-041: nothing fires today because no
# Kotlin binding can see a primary constructor. When Kotlin primary-
# constructor facts land, the first case must flip to expect while the
# patched form, the non-DTO component and the direct-return control stay
# reject.

test "rank1857: public configuration DTO declares the whole properties object as a serialized constructor property"
  lang kotlin
  file PublicConfigurationDTO.kt
  reject VYQL-SEC-041
  code
  ```
  import io.swagger.v3.oas.annotations.media.Schema
  import io.tolgee.configuration.tolgee.TolgeeProperties
  import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.CrossOrigin
  import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping
  import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestMapping
  import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController

  @RestController
  @CrossOrigin(origins = ["*"])
  @RequestMapping("/api/public/")
  class ConfigurationController(
    private val configuration: TolgeeProperties,
  ) {
    @GetMapping(value = ["configuration"])
    fun getPublicConfiguration(): PublicConfigurationDTO {
      return PublicConfigurationDTO(properties = configuration)
    }
  }

  class PublicConfigurationDTO(
    @Schema(hidden = true)
    val properties: TolgeeProperties,
  ) {
    val appName: String = properties.appName
    val maxUploadFileSize: Int = properties.maxUploadFileSize
    val recaptchaSiteKey: String = properties.recaptcha.siteKey
    val smtpHost: String = properties.smtp.host
  }
  ```

